# Ownership Tokens and API-client Ownership Demo
#
# This REST Client file is a guided walkthrough for the Configuration Service
# endpoints that manage ownership tokens and assign them to API clients.
#
# Run the requests from top to bottom in VS Code REST Client or another
# compatible .http client. Each request shows one business concept:
#
# 1. Get an administrator token for the Configuration Service.
# 2. Create the minimum vendor, data store, application, and API-client records
#    needed for a realistic demo.
# 3. Create ownership tokens in the tenant-wide ownership token catalog.
# 4. Assign those tokens to one API client through the ownership subresource.
# 5. Read the API client back and verify the ownership fields appear in both
#    item and collection responses.
# 6. Try optional validation examples to show how the API protects the model.
#
# Key idea for demo audiences:
# - Ownership tokens are labels that identify who owns data in DMS.
# - creatorOwnershipTokenId answers: "Which token will this API client stamp on
#   records it creates?"
# - ownershipTokenIds answers: "Which existing ownership tokens may this API
#   client read or modify?"
# - API-client create/update does not accept ownership fields directly. Use
#   /v3/apiClients/{id}/ownership so ownership assignment is explicit and easy
#   to audit.

@configPort=8081
@configBaseUrl=http://localhost:{{configPort}}

# These are the default local administrator credentials used by the
# Configuration Service E2E/local setup.
@sysAdminId=DmsConfigurationService
@sysAdminSecret=ValidClientSecret1234567890!Abcd

# Use a suffix so repeated demos create distinct records instead of colliding
# with data left by an earlier run.
@demoSuffix={{$randomInt 100000 999999}}

### ============================================================================
### STEP 1: Authenticate as a Configuration Service administrator
### ============================================================================
### The ownership endpoints are Management API endpoints. They require an
### administrator bearer token with the edfi_admin_api/full_access scope.
###
### Expected result: 200 OK with an access_token in the JSON response body.

# @name configTokenRequest
POST {{configBaseUrl}}/connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

client_id={{sysAdminId}}
&client_secret={{sysAdminSecret}}
&grant_type=client_credentials
&scope=edfi_admin_api/full_access

###
@configToken={{configTokenRequest.response.body.access_token}}


### ============================================================================
### STEP 2: Create demo setup data
### ============================================================================
### Ownership is assigned to an API client, and an API client belongs to an
### application. The application belongs to a vendor and references one or more
### data stores. These setup calls create that small hierarchy.

### Create a vendor
### A vendor represents the organization that owns one or more applications.
### Expected result: 201 Created with a Location header.
# @name createOwnershipDemoVendor
POST {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/vendors
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}

{
    "company": "Ownership Demo Vendor {{demoSuffix}}",
    "contactName": "Demo Administrator",
    "contactEmailAddress": "ownership-demo-{{demoSuffix}}@example.org",
    "namespacePrefixes": "uri://ownership-demo.example.org"
}

###
@vendorLocation={{createOwnershipDemoVendor.response.headers.location}}

### Read the vendor back so the demo can capture its numeric id
### The vendor POST returns the new resource URI in Location. This GET follows
### that URI and reads the id needed by the application create request.
# @name getOwnershipDemoVendor
GET {{vendorLocation}}
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}

###
@vendorId={{getOwnershipDemoVendor.response.body.id}}


### Create a data store
### A data store identifies the DMS database/instance an API client is allowed
### to use. This demo uses a harmless Test connection string because the point
### here is Configuration Service ownership metadata, not DMS data access.
### Expected result: 201 Created with an id in the response body.
# @name createOwnershipDemoDataStore
POST {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/dataStores
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}

{
    "dataStoreType": "Test",
    "name": "Ownership Demo Data Store {{demoSuffix}}",
    "connectionString": "Server=test;Database=OwnershipDemo{{demoSuffix}};"
}

###
@dataStoreId={{createOwnershipDemoDataStore.response.body.id}}


### Create an application
### An application groups one or more API clients under the same vendor,
### claim set, and data store access. The Configuration Service also creates
### default client credentials for the application, but the next request creates
### a named API client so the ownership demo is explicit.
### Expected result: 201 Created with application id, key, and secret.
# @name createOwnershipDemoApplication
POST {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/applications
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}

{
    "vendorId": {{vendorId}},
    "applicationName": "Ownership Demo Application {{demoSuffix}}",
    "claimSetName": "TestClaim01",
    "dataStoreIds": [{{dataStoreId}}]
}

###
@applicationId={{createOwnershipDemoApplication.response.body.id}}


### Create a named API client
### This is the credential-bearing client record that will receive ownership
### settings. isApproved=true means the client is enabled. dataStoreIds limits
### the data stores the client can use.
###
### Expected result: 201 Created. The response body includes:
### - id: internal numeric API-client id used by /v3/apiClients/{id}/ownership
### - key: client id string used by GET /v3/apiClients/{clientId}
### - secret: shown once so the integrator can authenticate to DMS later
# @name createOwnershipDemoApiClient
POST {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/apiClients
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}

{
    "applicationId": {{applicationId}},
    "name": "Ownership Demo API Client {{demoSuffix}}",
    "isApproved": true,
    "dataStoreIds": [{{dataStoreId}}]
}

###
@apiClientId={{createOwnershipDemoApiClient.response.body.id}}
@apiClientKey={{createOwnershipDemoApiClient.response.body.key}}


### ============================================================================
### STEP 3: Read the API client's ownership before assigning tokens
### ============================================================================
### New API clients start without configured ownership. That is represented as:
### - creatorOwnershipTokenId: null
### - ownershipTokenIds: []
###
### Expected result: 200 OK with the empty ownership representation.
# @name getInitialApiClientOwnership
GET {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/apiClients/{{apiClientId}}/ownership
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}


### ============================================================================
### STEP 4: Create ownership tokens in the catalog
### ============================================================================
### The catalog is tenant-wide. Creating a token only creates the label; it does
### not give any API client access until the token is assigned to that client.
###
### Description is required and limited to 50 characters.

### Create the token this API client will use when it creates records
### Expected result: 201 Created with Location: /v3/ownershipTokens/{id}.
# @name createCreatorOwnershipToken
POST {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/ownershipTokens
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}

{
    "description": "Creator Token {{demoSuffix}}"
}

###
@creatorOwnershipTokenLocation={{createCreatorOwnershipToken.response.headers.location}}

### Read the creator token back to capture its id
### POST intentionally returns the new resource URI instead of a response body.
### This GET makes the id visible for the later ownership assignment payload.
# @name getCreatorOwnershipToken
GET {{creatorOwnershipTokenLocation}}
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}

###
@creatorOwnershipTokenId={{getCreatorOwnershipToken.response.body.id}}


### Create the token this API client may read or modify
### A client can have many read/modify ownership tokens. This demo uses one so
### the relationship is easy to see in the response.
# @name createReadOwnershipToken
POST {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/ownershipTokens
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}

{
    "description": "Read Token {{demoSuffix}}"
}

###
@readOwnershipTokenLocation={{createReadOwnershipToken.response.headers.location}}

### Read the read/modify token back to capture its id
# @name getReadOwnershipToken
GET {{readOwnershipTokenLocation}}
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}

###
@readOwnershipTokenId={{getReadOwnershipToken.response.body.id}}


### List ownership tokens with ordering and paging
### This endpoint is useful for admin screens and audits. It returns catalog
### tokens only; it does not say which clients use each token.
###
### Supported demo query values:
### - orderBy=description sorts by the human-readable label.
### - direction=asc or direction=desc controls sort direction.
### - limit and offset page through larger catalogs.
# @name listOwnershipTokens
GET {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/ownershipTokens?orderBy=description&direction=asc&limit=25&offset=0
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}


### Update a token description
### Use this when an admin wants to rename a token without changing which API
### clients reference it. The route id and request body id must match.
###
### Expected result: 204 No Content. A later GET shows the new description.
# @name updateReadOwnershipToken
PUT {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/ownershipTokens/{{readOwnershipTokenId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}

{
    "id": {{readOwnershipTokenId}},
    "description": "Updated Read Token {{demoSuffix}}"
}


### Verify the token description changed
# @name getUpdatedReadOwnershipToken
GET {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/ownershipTokens/{{readOwnershipTokenId}}
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}


### ============================================================================
### STEP 5: Assign ownership tokens to the API client
### ============================================================================
### This is the central endpoint for the feature.
###
### PUT /v3/apiClients/{id}/ownership replaces the complete ownership
### representation for one API client. It is a full replacement, not a patch:
### any token omitted from ownershipTokenIds is removed from that client's
### read/modify list.
###
### Expected result: 204 No Content.
# @name replaceApiClientOwnership
PUT {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/apiClients/{{apiClientId}}/ownership
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}

{
    "apiClientId": {{apiClientId}},
    "creatorOwnershipTokenId": {{creatorOwnershipTokenId}},
    "ownershipTokenIds": [{{readOwnershipTokenId}}]
}


### Read the ownership subresource after replacement
### This is the most direct way for an admin tool to answer:
### "What ownership configuration does this API client have right now?"
###
### Expected result:
### {
###   "apiClientId": <the client id>,
###   "creatorOwnershipTokenId": <creator token id>,
###   "ownershipTokenIds": [<read token id>]
### }
# @name getAssignedApiClientOwnership
GET {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/apiClients/{{apiClientId}}/ownership
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}


### ============================================================================
### STEP 6: Verify ownership appears on API-client read endpoints
### ============================================================================
### The API-client read endpoints include the ownership columns so admin tools
### do not need a second request just to display the configured ownership.

### Read one API client by its client id string
### The route uses the public client id/key, not the internal numeric id.
### Expected result includes creatorOwnershipTokenId and ownershipTokenIds.
# @name getApiClientByClientId
GET {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/apiClients/{{apiClientKey}}
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}


### Read the API-client collection
### This demonstrates that list responses expose the same ownership fields as
### the single-item response. That matters for admin grids and demo screens
### because they can show ownership without opening each client.
# @name listApiClientsWithOwnership
GET {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/apiClients?offset=0&limit=25
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}


### ============================================================================
### STEP 7: Clear the API client's ownership
### ============================================================================
### Sending null for creatorOwnershipTokenId and an empty ownershipTokenIds array
### removes ownership settings from this API client. This is also a full
### replacement, so the previous creator/read tokens remain in the catalog but
### are no longer assigned to this API client.
###
### Expected result: 204 No Content.
# @name clearApiClientOwnership
PUT {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/apiClients/{{apiClientId}}/ownership
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}

{
    "apiClientId": {{apiClientId}},
    "creatorOwnershipTokenId": null,
    "ownershipTokenIds": []
}


### Verify ownership was cleared
### Expected result has creatorOwnershipTokenId=null and ownershipTokenIds=[].
# @name getClearedApiClientOwnership
GET {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/apiClients/{{apiClientId}}/ownership
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}


### ============================================================================
### OPTIONAL VALIDATION EXAMPLES
### ============================================================================
### These requests intentionally produce errors. Use them in demos to explain
### how the API prevents ambiguous ownership changes.

### Validation: route id and body apiClientId must match
### Expected result: 400 Bad Request. The server rejects the request before
### changing anything because it cannot safely know which API client you meant.
# @name rejectMismatchedApiClientOwnershipId
PUT {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/apiClients/{{apiClientId}}/ownership
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}

{
    "apiClientId": 999999,
    "creatorOwnershipTokenId": null,
    "ownershipTokenIds": []
}


### Validation: ownership fields are not accepted on API-client update
### Expected result: 400 Bad Request. Ownership must be managed through the
### ownership subresource so normal client edits do not accidentally change
### data ownership permissions.
# @name rejectOwnershipFieldsOnApiClientUpdate
PUT {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/apiClients/{{apiClientId}}
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}

{
    "id": {{apiClientId}},
    "applicationId": {{applicationId}},
    "name": "Ownership Demo API Client {{demoSuffix}}",
    "isApproved": true,
    "creatorOwnershipTokenId": {{creatorOwnershipTokenId}},
    "ownershipTokenIds": [{{readOwnershipTokenId}}],
    "dataStoreIds": [{{dataStoreId}}]
}


### Validation: referenced ownership tokens must exist
### Expected result: 409 Conflict. The payload shape is valid, but token 32767
### has not been created in this tenant, so assigning it would create a broken
### reference.
# @name rejectMissingOwnershipToken
PUT {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/apiClients/{{apiClientId}}/ownership
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}

{
    "apiClientId": {{apiClientId}},
    "creatorOwnershipTokenId": 32767,
    "ownershipTokenIds": []
}


### Validation: ownershipTokenIds cannot contain duplicates
### Expected result: 400 Bad Request. Duplicate read/modify tokens would not add
### access, so the API rejects them as invalid input.
# @name rejectDuplicateOwnershipTokenIds
PUT {{configBaseUrl}}/v3/apiClients/{{apiClientId}}/ownership
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: bearer {{configToken}}

{
    "apiClientId": {{apiClientId}},
    "creatorOwnershipTokenId": null,
    "ownershipTokenIds": [{{readOwnershipTokenId}}, {{readOwnershipTokenId}}]
}
